---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { languagePaths } from '../../lib/locales';
---
<BaseLayout title="About ShippingMath" description="Who builds ShippingMath, why it exists and how the free shipping tools are checked." canonicalPath="/about/" languagePaths={languagePaths.about}>
  <header class="tool-hero"><span class="eyebrow">About the project</span><h1>Shipping math should lead to a decision.</h1><p>ShippingMath turns package measurements into plain-language cost and margin signals for sellers everywhere.</p></header>
  <article class="content-section">
    <h2>Who builds it</h2>
    <p>ShippingMath is built and maintained by Harsh Parmar, an independent developer. I started it after seeing how often small online sellers lose margin to dimensional weight without noticing, and how few calculators explain why a package was billed the way it was.</p>
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>Many calculators stop at a formula. ShippingMath keeps scale weight, dimensional weight, billable weight, package volume and margin together so a seller can decide whether to change the carton, the price or the shipping charge.</p>
    <h2>How the rules are checked</h2>
    <p>Carrier rules change. Each preset is checked against the carriers' published rules, and the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> lists the sources and the date of the last review. If you spot a rule that has changed, please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>.</p>
    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>ShippingMath is independent planning software. It is not affiliated with any carrier, and it is not a postage quote, an accounting system or a substitute for your current carrier agreement.</p>
    <h2>Free first</h2>
    <p>The tools are free and do not require registration. Paid features will only be considered if users repeatedly need saved catalogues, larger workflows, integrations or team reporting.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
